# TASK — one kept number, a model fitted here

You are a researcher new to this lab repository. Work only inside this
directory; use `.venv/bin/python`. Every `.venv/bin/…` path is relative to
the repo root: run commands from there, not from an area directory. You
may commit (never push). Do not ask questions; make reasonable choices and
note them in your report.

**The question.** Does a random forest on Morgan fingerprints predict
drug-induced liver injury (DILI) concern better than the majority class?
The data are in `data/raw/dili/train.csv`: 923 molecules with
`standardised_smiles` and the label `vDILIConcern` (`vMost-DILI-Concern`
and `vLess-DILI-Concern` are DILI; `vNo-DILI-Concern` and `sider_inactive`
are not). `scikit-learn`, `pandas`, `numpy` and `rdkit` are installed in
`.venv`; there is no network. The kept number is the registered lane's
**out-of-fold accuracy** over the labelled molecules, in the run's
`metrics.json` under the key `<metric_key>`, alongside whatever the
repo's rules demand of a number that judges a fitted model (a null lane
in the same run, the registered criterion against it, the admissibility
condition). A fit of a few hundred trees on 923 molecules takes well
under a minute. The area `experiments/<area>/` is already stamped for
it. <tier>

Follow the repo's own rules: `AGENTS.md` is in your context already (as a
session loads it), the lab standard holds the long form
(`python -m labkit.formats.rules show LAB-nnn`) — read what you need, no more.

**The log** matters as much as the number. ONE line per step, BEFORE the
step, in `<log>`:

    HH:MM:SS · <tag> · <the step, in a few words>

`.venv/bin/usability log <tag> "<the step>" --log <log> -- <command>` writes
the line with the current time and then runs the command; without `--` it
only logs (a read or an edit you do with a tool). tags: `read` (a file you
open) · `run` (a command) · `edit` (a file you write) · `unsure` (the rules
leave you guessing) · `gate` (a check, hook or refusal makes you change
course). Every step gets a line, written before the step, never batched
afterwards; the log is the measurement.

**Report** when done, with these headings: (1) the number, the run_id and
where the number lives · (2) files read with line counts, commands run,
edits made, wall-clock · (3) the three biggest time sinks, each with the
